From: Oever Gonzalez <software@notengobattery.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: airoha: npu: bound the mailbox poll

__airoha_npu_send_msg() waits for the NPU to acknowledge a mailbox
message with regmap_read_poll_timeout_atomic() and a 100 second timeout,
inside spin_lock_bh(), and the retry loop added for rejected messages
runs the whole thing three times. An NPU
that stops answering therefore spins a CPU with bottom halves disabled
for up to five minutes per message.

That is not a theoretical window. The wireless driver polls this mailbox
from its reset path, and the PPE calls it from foe_commit in atomic
context, so a single unanswered message takes the machine down with it:
unrelated processes pile up in uninterruptible sleep, "reboot -f" hangs
because it cannot complete device shutdown, and only sysrq gets out.

Nothing here is slow when it works -- the hot path is a flow commit, and
the poll interval is 100 us. Give it 100 ms, still a thousand times the
expected latency, and let the retry loop cover a transient. Worst
case goes from five minutes to under a second, and a dead NPU surfaces
as the -ETIMEDOUT its callers already handle.

--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_npu.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_npu.c
@@ -29,6 +29,7 @@
  * is 16 bytes (INODE_TXRX_REG_ADDR). Keep headroom for future commands.
  */
 #define AIROHA_NPU_MBOX_SIZE			256
+#define AIROHA_NPU_MBOX_TIMEOUT_US		(100 * USEC_PER_MSEC)
 
 #define REG_NPU_LOCAL_SRAM		0x0
 
@@ -203,7 +204,7 @@
 	ret = regmap_read_poll_timeout_atomic(npu->regmap,
 					      REG_CR_MBQ0_CTRL(3) + offset,
 					      val, (val & MBOX_MSG_DONE),
-					      100, 100 * MSEC_PER_SEC);
+					      100, AIROHA_NPU_MBOX_TIMEOUT_US);
 	if (!ret && FIELD_GET(MBOX_MSG_STATUS, val) != NPU_MBOX_SUCCESS)
 		ret = -EINVAL;
 
